# Git commits and pushes — no Cursor attribution

When creating commits, amending, or pushing (including PR titles/bodies opened as part of push):

- **Do not** include the word `Cursor` or `cursor` anywhere in commit messages (subject or body).
- **Do not** add trailers or metadata that reference Cursor (e.g. `Co-authored-by`, `Signed-off-by`, `--author`, or similar).
- **Do not** mention Cursor, the IDE, or AI assistance in push-related text (PR title, PR description, release notes tied to the push).

Write commit messages as normal human/project contributions: focus on **why** the change exists, match this repo’s existing commit style, and use only product/code terms (e.g. BuildLens, module names).

**Examples**

```text
# Bad
feat: settings tiles (via Cursor)

# Bad
fix: hydration

Co-authored-by: Cursor <noreply@cursor.com>

# Good
feat (settings): widen layout and add section navigation tiles
```
